+# Agent Note: Authorize images in tool result metadata
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-29-tool-result-metadata-images.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+A tool can keep its model-facing result text-only while placing client presentation blocks in `tool/result.data.meta.content`. An image referenced only by those blocks was durable in the session log but unavailable through `session.attachment`, and a session-log export omitted its object. The client could therefore identify a GUI step screenshot without being able to load or export it.
+
+## Decision
+
+Session attachment authorization and session-log export scan `tool/result.data.meta.content` with the same image-block rules used for other event content carriers. The reference remains subject to session ownership and attachment-store verification; metadata does not grant access to an object that the session does not reference.
+
+The macOS task tools project retained step screenshots through `presentationMeta` while their model-facing result stays text-only. Their declared image-reference output accepts the attachment service's complete normalized reference, including optional `originalDimensions`, so schema validation and client presentation consume the same durable object.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Place screenshots in the model-facing result content.** Rejected because the driving model does not need pixels after the GUI model makes the action decision, and text-only model routes can reject image result blocks.
+
+**Issue a separate file path or bearer URL for each screenshot.** Rejected because paths and URLs bypass session-scoped authorization, content-addressed verification, and portable session export.
+
+## Consequences
+
+An authorized session reader can load and export images used only by a tool's client presentation. GUI step screenshots add attachment objects and metadata to durable history when enabled, but they do not add images to the driving model request. Any producer that places image blocks in `presentationMeta` must use the standard attachment-reference shape.
